## What Each Defense Does

The protected server layers three independent controls, because no single defense is sufficient on its own:

`SameSite=Strict` on the session cookie tells the browser not to send the cookie on any cross-site request. This stops the attack at the browser level before the request even reaches the server. The limitation is that older browsers don't support it.

The synchronizer token pattern (csurf) embeds a secret token in the session and requires it to be echoed back in every state-changing request. An attacker on a different origin can't read the token due to the same-origin policy, so they can't forge a valid request.

Origin/Referer header validation is a server-side check that rejects requests whose `Origin` or `Referer` header doesn't match the expected domain. This is a defense-in-depth measure for cases where the other two controls are misconfigured or bypassed.
